# Web Push

Web Push puts a notification on the user's screen through the browser
itself, the way a native app's notifications appear, even when no tab of
your app is open. `howdy/notifications/web_push` sends them for you with
the open standards every major browser supports, so there is no
third-party service to sign up for.

## Keys

Push services identify your app by a key pair, called VAPID keys. Make one
pair, once:

```sh
gleam run -m howdy/notifications/web_push
VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=BO2l…
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=x8Cq…
```

Keep them in the environment like any other secret. Browsers subscribe
with the public key, so if the keys change every subscription stops
working and users have to turn push on again.

## Setting up

```gleam
import howdy/notifications/web_push

let assert Ok(public) = env.get("VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY")
let assert Ok(private) = env.get("VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY")
let assert Ok(keys) = web_push.keys(public:, private:)

let push = web_push.new(notifier, keys, contact: "mailto:ops@example.com")
let notifier = notifications.with_push(notifier, web_push.deliver(push))

howdy.new()
|> howdy.controller(notifications.routes(notifier, at: "/notifications", identify:))
|> howdy.controller(web_push.routes(push, at: "/push", identify:))
```

`contact` is how a push service can reach whoever runs the app if
something goes wrong: a `mailto:` or `https:` URL, and `new` refuses
anything else. Use a real address. Apple's push service refuses every
notification whose contact is missing, not one of these, or on `localhost`,
while Chrome's and Firefox's keep working, so the mistake only shows on
Safari. Push is sent by the same
[jobs](/docs/notifications/delivery#Sending-them) as email, so a runner
with `notifications.handle_jobs(notifier)` sends it too.

`web_push.routes` serves what browsers need:

- `GET /push/worker.js`, the service worker that shows each notification
  and opens its link when clicked
- `GET /push/key`, the public key
- `POST /push/subscriptions`, to keep a browser's subscription for the
  signed-in user
- `DELETE /push/subscriptions`, to forget one

## Turning it on

Browsers only allow push after the user agrees, and the request must come
from something the user clicked. Give the bell the routes' path and its
panel shows a button for it, in browsers that support push:

```gleam
bell.new(id: "inbox", source: "/notifications")
|> bell.push(at: "/push")
|> bell.view
```

Clicking it asks for permission, registers the service worker, subscribes
with the browser's push service and saves the subscription. Clicking again
turns it off. If the user has blocked notifications for the site, the panel
says so, since only the browser's settings can undo that.

A user turns push on separately in each browser they use. Each kind's
`push` preference then decides which notifications go to them.

## What is sent

Each notification goes to every browser the user turned push on in. It is
encrypted for that browser alone (RFC 8291), so the push service in the
middle cannot read it, and signed with your keys (RFC 8292). The service
worker shows the title and body, tagged with the notification's ID, and
opens the link when it is clicked, focusing a tab that is already there.

When a push service says a subscription has gone, because the user turned
notifications off or the browser was reset, it is deleted. When it asks to
be tried later, the push job retries the whole notification; browsers show a
notification once per tag, so the ones that already had it do not show it
twice.

## Which push services

The app sends to the address a browser's subscription gives, so a
subscription could name any server. Only the push services of Chrome,
Chromium, Edge, Firefox and Safari are accepted, so your app cannot be made
to send requests anywhere else. `allow_endpoints` widens or replaces the
list, for a self-hosted push service:

```gleam
web_push.allow_endpoints(push, fn(endpoint) {
  web_push.known_push_service(endpoint)
  || endpoint.host == Some("push.example.com")
})
```

## Other push services

Web Push is one way to push. To push to phones too, through Firebase Cloud
Messaging, Apple's push service or one such as Expo, give the notifier a
function for each, by name:

```gleam
notifier
|> notifications.with_push_service("web", web_push.deliver(push))
|> notifications.with_push_service("phones", send_to_phones)
```

`send_to_phones` is given each `Notification` to push, finds the user's
devices itself, and says what became of it: `Delivered`, `Retry(reason)`
when the service is down, or `GiveUp(reason)` when it never will work.
`with_push` is the service named `"default"`.

A notification the user wants pushed goes to every service, each in a job
of its own, so one service that is down is retried without sending the
others again. Each kind's `push` preference turns them all on or off; a
user picks devices by turning push on in each one. Keep a service's name
once notifications have gone out with it: jobs waiting for a name the
notifier no longer has fail.

## Limits

- Push needs HTTPS, except on `localhost`.
- Safari on iOS only offers push to web apps added to the home screen.
- The browser decides how notifications look, and a user can turn them off
  for your site at any time.
